rknpu: pin the module for anything that outlives its fd

remove() frees rknpu_dev - it is devm memory - and drops the driver's DRM
reference, but neither waits for objects that are still reachable. A GEM
object released after that, or a fence held by another driver, reaches back
into freed memory.

The general fix for that is hot-unplug: drm_dev_unplug, drm_dev_enter/exit
around every hardware path, and GEM destruction split so the final free needs
no power and no domain switch. On this part the last of those means replacing
dma_alloc_attrs - which fuses allocation with IOMMU mapping - with hand-rolled
page and domain management across every GEM path, which is the riskiest code
in the driver, to support unplugging an NPU that is soldered to the board and
described by the base device tree.

Enforce the invariant the hardware actually has instead: teardown does not
begin while anything the driver owns is alive.

Most of that already holds and costs nothing. An open DRM fd pins the module
through DEFINE_DRM_GEM_FOPS, an mmap holds that file, an exported dma-buf
inherits the same owner through drm_gem_prime_export, the misc device sets
one, and procfs entries are rundown-protected by the proc core. Three things
were left:

  - Fences. dma_fence_ops has no owner and sync_file hands the fence to
    anyone. A fence also takes its lock from fence_ctx, which is devm memory,
    so one outliving remove() would lock through a freed allocation.

  - Jobs. A nonblocking submit returns while the job and its cleanup work
    continue, by which time the fd may be gone.

  - Deferred GEM objects. Reachable from neither an fd nor a dma-buf: the
    handle is gone and the destroy that would have freed it could not run, so
    the driver's own list is the only thing holding them. The reference is
    dropped by the destroy that actually frees, not by the one that defers.

Sysfs unbind is removed with suppress_bind_attrs, because it starts teardown
at a moment userspace picks and no reference count inside the driver can
refuse it. The device is not removable in any real sense.

rmmod with any of these alive now returns EBUSY instead of leaving a
dangling pointer. Counters say which kind is outstanding if the invariant is
ever broken, since the module refcount alone cannot.

Hot-unplug and overlay-created NPU devices are not supported.

Signed-off-by: Isaiah DeRose-Wilson <isaiahdw@gmail.com>

--- a/rknpu_drv.c
+++ b/rknpu_drv.c
@@ -326,6 +326,24 @@
 static int rknpu_power_on(struct rknpu_device *rknpu_dev);
 static int rknpu_power_off(struct rknpu_device *rknpu_dev);
 
+/*
+ * Hold the module for an object that can outlive the fd that made it.
+ *
+ * The caller is always inside an ioctl on an open fd, which already pins the
+ * module, so the count cannot be going to zero here and __module_get is safe.
+ */
+void rknpu_pin_module(atomic_t *live)
+{
+	__module_get(THIS_MODULE);
+	atomic_inc(live);
+}
+
+void rknpu_unpin_module(atomic_t *live)
+{
+	atomic_dec(live);
+	module_put(THIS_MODULE);
+}
+
 static void rknpu_devfreq_floor(struct rknpu_device *rknpu_dev, bool on)
 {
 	int ret = rknpu_devfreq_boost(rknpu_dev, on);
@@ -1929,6 +1947,21 @@
 		rknpu_gem_flush_deferred(rknpu_dev, false);
 	}
 
+	/*
+	 * Every object that can outlive its fd holds a module reference, and
+	 * this only runs once the last one is gone, so all three are zero. A
+	 * non-zero count means a reference was taken and never dropped: the
+	 * object is about to point into freed memory and nothing here can fix
+	 * that, so name it instead of leaving a later crash to explain it.
+	 */
+	WARN(atomic_read(&rknpu_dev->live_gem) ||
+		     atomic_read(&rknpu_dev->live_jobs) ||
+		     atomic_read(&rknpu_dev->live_fences),
+	     "rknpu: teardown with %d gem, %d job(s), %d fence(s) still live\n",
+	     atomic_read(&rknpu_dev->live_gem),
+	     atomic_read(&rknpu_dev->live_jobs),
+	     atomic_read(&rknpu_dev->live_fences));
+
 	mutex_lock(&rknpu_dev->power_lock);
 	if (READ_ONCE(rknpu_dev->power_wedged)) {
 		powered = true;
@@ -2058,6 +2091,13 @@
